#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:3
msgid "Testing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:5
msgid ""
"CodeIgniter has been built to make testing both the framework and your "
"application as simple as possible. Support for `PHPUnit "
"<https://phpunit.de/>`__ is built in, and the framework provides a number"
" of convenient helper methods to make testing every aspect of your "
"application as painless as possible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:15
msgid "System Set Up"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:18
msgid "Installing PHPUnit"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:20
msgid ""
"CodeIgniter uses `PHPUnit <https://phpunit.de/>`__ as the basis for all "
"of its testing. There are two ways to install PHPUnit to use within your "
"system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:24
msgid "Composer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:26
msgid ""
"The recommended method is to install it in your project using `Composer "
"<https://getcomposer.org/>`__. While it's possible to install it globally"
" we do not recommend it, since it can cause compatibility issues with "
"other projects on your system as time goes on."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:30
msgid ""
"Ensure that you have Composer installed on your system. From the project "
"root (the directory that contains the application and system directories)"
" type the following from the command line:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:37
msgid ""
"This will install the correct version for your current PHP version. Once "
"that is done, you can run all of the tests for this project by typing:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:44
msgid "If you are using Windows, use the following command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:51
msgid "Phar"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:53
msgid ""
"The other option is to download the .phar file from the `PHPUnit "
"<https://phpunit.de/getting-started/phpunit-9.html>`__ site. This is a "
"standalone file that should be placed within your project root."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:58
msgid "Testing Your Application"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:61
msgid "PHPUnit Configuration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:63
msgid ""
"In your CodeIgniter project root, there is the ``phpunit.dist.xml`` file."
" This controls unit testing of your application. If you provide your own "
"``phpunit.xml``, it will over-ride this."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:67
msgid ""
"By default, test files are placed under the **tests** directory in the "
"project root."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:70
msgid "The Test Class"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:72
msgid ""
"In order to take advantage of the additional tools provided, your tests "
"must extend ``CodeIgniter\\Test\\CIUnitTestCase``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:75
msgid ""
"There are no rules for how test files must be placed. However, we "
"recommend that you establish placement rules in advance so that you can "
"quickly understand where the test files are located."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:79
msgid ""
"In this document, the test files corresponding to the classes in the "
"**app** directory will be placed in the **tests/app** directory. To test "
"a new library, **app/Libraries/Foo.php**, you would create a new file at "
"**tests/app/Libraries/FooTest.php**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:86
msgid ""
"To test one of your models, **app/Models/UserModel.php**, you might end "
"up with something like this in **tests/app/Models/UserModelTest.php**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:91
msgid ""
"You can create any directory structure that fits your testing "
"style/needs. When namespacing the test classes, remember that the **app**"
" directory is the root of the ``App`` namespace, so any classes you use "
"must have the correct namespace relative to ``App``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:95
msgid ""
"Namespaces are not strictly required for test classes, but they are "
"helpful to ensure no class names collide."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:97
msgid ""
"When testing database results, you must use the :doc:`DatabaseTestTrait "
"<database>` in your class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:100
msgid "Staging"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:102
msgid ""
"Most tests require some preparation in order to run correctly. PHPUnit's "
"``TestCase`` provides four methods to help with staging and clean up::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:111
msgid ""
"The static methods ``setUpBeforeClass()`` and ``tearDownAfterClass()`` "
"run before and after the entire test case, whereas the protected methods "
"``setUp()`` and ``tearDown()`` run between each test."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:114
msgid ""
"If you implement any of these special functions, make sure you run their "
"parent as well so extended test cases do not interfere with staging:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:122
msgid "Traits"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:124
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"A common way to enhance your tests is using traits to consolidate staging"
" across different test cases. ``CIUnitTestCase`` will detect any class "
"traits and look for staging methods to run named for the trait itself "
"(i.e. `setUp{NameOfTrait}()` and `tearDown{NameOfTrait}()`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/overview.rst:128
msgid ""
"For example, if you needed to add authentication to some of your test "
"cases you could create an authentication trait with a set up method to "
"fake a logged in user:"
msgstr ""

